t volume/popularity explanations. Bucketed timing (steps 03-04) shows 798 active only near ~11000, ~25600 and ~34000, while 131's events are at ~10600, 17000-19900, ~25000, ~26200 and ~32200. Most of 798's activity is thousands of units away from any 131 event (and 131's ~10600 event precedes 798 entirely), so no cascade is possible there. The single viable candidate is 798's event in [25302,25901] followed by 131's event in [25902,26501] - an inter-event gap that may or may not fall within the 600-unit limit, and whose intermediate hop (presumably via 389) was never timed. Evidence is therefore genuinely inconclusive rather than clearly present or absent."}, "raw_final_response": "## Analysis\n\n**Step 01 (static structure):** 798 and 131 are both attached to the mega-hub 389 (edges `389\u2013798` and `389\u2013131`), so a 2-hop connection exists *when time is ignored*. This is exactly the confounder the task warns about \u2014 hub 389 touches hundreds of nodes, so static reachability is nearly guaranteed and carries no evidence.\n\n**Step 02 (volume control):** Both endpoints are very sparse \u2014 798 has only 4 events / 4 distinct partners, 131 has 7 events / 7 partners. Neither is a \"popular\" node, so any conclusion must rest on timing, not volume.\n\n**Steps 03\u201304 (temporal profiles, 600-unit buckets):**\n- 798 fires only in three buckets: `[10902,11501]` (\u00d72), `[25302,25901]`, `[33702,34301]`.\n- 131 fires in: `[10302,10901]`, `[16902,17501]`, `[17502,18101]`, `[19302,19901]`, `[24702,25301]`, `[25902,26501]`, `[31902,32501]`.\n\nConsequences:\n- 131's only activity before 798's first event (`[10302,10901]`) cannot be caused by it.\n- After 798's first burst (~11000), 131 is silent for \u22655400 units \u2014 far beyond one 600-unit hop, so any influence would need a long unverified relay chain.\n- After 798's last event (`[33702,34301]`), 131 never fires again.\n- **One candidate remains:** 798 fires in `[25302,25901]` and 131 fires in the immediately following bucket `[25902,26501]`. The inter-event gap could be anywhere from ~1 to ~1199 units, so this is *consistent with* a 1\u20132 hop time-respecting relay (plausibly via 389), but it is not demonstrated: I never established the direction or the timestamp of a 389\u2192131 event relative to a 798\u2192389 event.\n\nI exhausted my call budget on structure and coarse timing, so the decisive middle link is unresolved: the evidence neither exhibits a verified \u2264600-unit chain nor excludes one.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_d7716db7\",\n  \"decision\": \"insufficient\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"cascade\",\n  \"entities\": [798, 131, 389],\n  \"window\": [4302, 42701],\n  \"confidence\": 0.45,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_03\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_04\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_01\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_02\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"}\n  ],\n  \"narrative\": \"Static 2-hop connectivity 798-389-131 exists but is uninformative: 389 is a mega-hub (step_01), and both endpoints are low-volume (4 and 7 events, step_02), ruling out volume/popularity explanations. Bucketed timing (steps 03-04) shows 798 active only near ~11000, ~25600 and ~34000, while 131's events are at ~10600, 17000-19900, ~25000, ~26200 and ~32200. Most of 798's activity is thousands of units away from any 131 event (and 131's ~10600 event precedes 798 entirely), so no cascade is possible there. The single viable candidate is 798's event in [25302,25901] followed by 131's event in [25902,26501] - an inter-event gap that may or may not fall within the 600-unit limit, and whose intermediate hop (presumably via 389) was never timed. Evidence is therefore genuinely inconclusive rather than clearly present or absent.\"\n}\n```", "total_input_tokens": 16136, "total_output_tokens": 5852, "total_tokens": 21988, "total_latency_ms": 78891, "llm_calls": 3, "schema_repairs": 0, "status": "success", "error": ""}